{
  "dom_id": "step-q117261",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191213/assets/23794678967f5b2cd1d409f116a9083aa60f942737ad41fa960e91263691f8c7.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ is the bisector of $∠COA.$ If $m∠BOA={127}^{∘}$ and $m∠BOD={75}^{∘},$ find $m∠BOC.$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191213/assets/72c0047a8d0b513a5159d31addf0e4c0c2eeefe0e73ceeadf8e154c37f583039.png)\n\nBy the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠DOA & =m∠BOA-m∠BOD \\\\  & ={127}^{∘}-{75}^{∘} \\\\  & ={52}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠DOA=m∠COD={52}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOC & =m∠BOD-m∠COD \\\\  & ={75}^{∘}-{52}^{∘} \\\\  & ={23}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
